## 5️⃣ VERY IMPORTANT QUESTION YOU ASKED

### 👉 “Is there anything in Spring which is NOT in Spring Boot?”

### ✅ Correct answer (this shows maturity):

> **NO. Spring Boot does NOT remove any Spring feature.**

Spring Boot includes **ALL Spring features**.

What Spring Boot does:

* Pre-configures them
* Simplifies usage
* Enables them automatically when needed

You can still:

* Write XML configs
* Define beans manually
* Customize everything
* Disable auto-configuration

👉 Spring Boot is **Spring + convenience**, not limitation.
---
## 6️⃣ What Spring has that Boot “hides” (this is subtle but important)

Spring Boot **hides complexity**, it does NOT remove capability.

Examples:

* You don’t see `DispatcherServlet` → but it exists
* You don’t configure `DataSource` → but it exists
* You don’t define `TransactionManager` → but it exists

Interview line:

> “Spring Boot abstracts configuration complexity but the underlying Spring mechanisms
remain the same.”

---
## 7️⃣ When should you NOT use Spring Boot?

This is where opinion matters 👇

❌ Avoid Spring Boot when:

* You are writing a **library**, not an application
* You want **full manual control** of everything
* You are integrating into an existing legacy Spring XML system
* You are forced to deploy only WARs in corporate app servers

✅ Use Spring Boot when:

* Microservices
* REST APIs
* Cloud / Kubernetes
* Faster development
* Independent deployment

👉 **90% of modern projects should use Spring Boot**
---
## 8️⃣ Why interviewers get confused

Many interviewers:

* Never worked on classic Spring
* Think Spring Boot is “new Spring”
* Don’t understand auto-configuration internals

So they ask:

> “Difference between Spring and Spring Boot?”

What they expect:

* Less configuration
* Embedded server
* Starters
* Auto config

But what they *rarely hear*:

> “Spring Boot is built on top of Spring and does not replace it.”

That statement instantly sets you apart.
